turns.content:
JavaScript has **no built-in `Array.prototype.shuffle()` method**. If you’ve encountered one, it comes from a library or custom code; whether it mutates the array depends on that implementation.

For a **new shuffled array**, you can copy it and apply a Fisher–Yates shuffle:

```js
function toShuffled(array) {
  const result = [...array];

  for (let i = result.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [result[i], result[j]] = [result[j], result[i]];
  }

  return result;
}
```

This leaves the original array unchanged. Avoid `array.sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5)`: it mutates the array and produces a biased shuffle.
